I've implemented the CommercePriceListAccountRel permission logic that will check
```
                CommercePriceList commercePriceList =
			_commercePriceListLocalService.fetchCommercePriceList(
				commercePriceListAccountRel.getCommercePriceListId());

		if (commercePriceList == null) {
			return false;
		}

		return permissionChecker.hasPermission(
			commercePriceList.getGroupId(), CommercePriceList.class.getName(),
			commercePriceList.getCommercePriceListId(), ActionKeys.UPDATE);
```

Then in the API ResouceImpl I could use the CommercePriceListAccountRelId to check against the CommercePriceListAccount model permissions
```
                             return addAction(
					"DELETE",
					commercePriceListAccountRel
						.getCommercePriceListAccountRelId(),
					"deletePriceListAccount",
					commercePriceListAccountRel.getUserId(),
					"com.liferay.commerce.price.list.model.CommercePriceListAccountRel",
					// The groupId might come from somewhere else to be more efficient
					commercePriceList.getGroupId());
```

## Hey, I just made a Pull Request!

<!-- Please describe what you added, and add a screenshot if possible.
     That makes it easier to understand the change so we can :shipit: faster. -->
This PR adds support for a new authentication provider - an AWS ALB OIDC reverse proxy. Since all authentication and authorization is managed upstream of Backstage, it only implements the refresh method. 

The change needing the most scrutiny in this PR is the addition of a `IdentityResolver` function to AuthProviderFactoryOptions. This pushes the responsibility of mapping authentication information to an identity into the individual backstage applications. This is mostly done to prevent scenarios like #3535 , but also because we simply don't have a standard identifier that comes down on the JWT from the ALB - it's likely to vary depending on the OIDC provider (i.e. if you're using the Microsoft Azure AD oidc provider, the sub claim will give a unique ID, but it's not actually linkable to the user ID in AAD)

One notable component missing from this PR is a UI component that triggers the 'login'. We use something like this:
```jsx
class DummySignInComponent extends React.Component<SignInPageProps, any> {
    constructor(props: any) {
        super(props)
        fetch(`${window.location.origin  }/api/auth/awsalb/refresh`).then(data => data.json()).then((data) => {
            props.onResult({userId: data.backstageIdentity.id, profile: data.profile})
        })
    }

    render() {
        return (
            <div />
        );
    }
}
```
to integrate our UI with this processor. I'm open to including it in the PR, but I know from previous issues that Spotify has one internally as well - maybe there's opportunity there for re-use across providers?

